WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.160
Every production loop should have resource and behavior limits.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:10.420
A loop that can continue indefinitely is not operationally reliable, even when each individual step looks reasonable.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:16.400
Without explicit budgets, a task can consume tokens, money, tool capacity, human attention,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.380
and wall-clock time while creating no justified path to completion.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:28.280
Budgets may include maximum iterations, elapsed time, model tokens, monetary cost, tool calls, retries per action,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.020
files or records changed, consecutive failures, and actions without measurable progress.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.560
These limits are not merely accounting controls.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:43.820
They define when the system must pause, escalate, or stop because continuing would no longer be justified by evidence.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.600
A budget makes the cost of uncertainty visible instead of letting it accumulate silently.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.820
Retries need classification.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.040
Repeating an identical action may be appropriate for a temporary network error.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.180
Invalid arguments should be corrected.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.560
Permission denials should not be bypassed.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:04.180
Policy blocks should terminate or request approval rather than trigger creative evasion.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:11.040
A new strategy should be based on new evidence or a narrower hypothesis, not on impatience with the previous result.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.820
No-progress detection prevents oscillation.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.720
Warning signs include repeating the same action, alternating between two incompatible outputs,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:25.620
reverting and recreating the same patch, rerunning an evaluation without changing relevant inputs,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:32.080
producing longer explanations without new evidence, or consuming budget while acceptance criteria remain unchanged.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.980
These signals should affect state, not disappear into the transcript.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.360
Recovery begins before damage occurs.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:44.340
Before a high-impact change, record the current state and decide how to reverse, compensate for,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.920
or prevent the effect.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.020
Not every external action can be rolled back, which is why prevention and approval gates matter.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.920
When the coding loop stops after several evidence-based repair attempts,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:03.060
its terminal report should include final status, stop reason, evidence, changes, remaining uncertainty, budget used,

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.280
and a recommended human next step.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.300
That report should be useful to the person who takes over, not merely a graceful goodbye.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:15.560
Safe termination is a successful engineering outcome when completion cannot be justified.
